  {"type": "split", "h2": "Adding a heat pump to the ducts in your Stoney Creek home", "photo": "in", "reverse": False,
   "html": "<p>Plenty of 1960s to 1980s houses below the escarpment already have a full set of ducts, a furnace in the basement and a central AC out back. That's good news. A central cold-climate heat pump replaces the old AC outside and connects to the same ductwork, so you get heating and cooling from one outdoor unit without opening a single wall.</p>"
           "<p>The catch is airflow. A heat pump delivers warm air at a lower temperature than a gas furnace, so it needs to move a bit more air to do the same job. Older ducts were often sized tight. Before we quote, we measure static pressure and look at the return side, because a choked return is the most common reason a heat pump feels weak.</p>"
           "<p>Newer subdivisions on the upper level usually have better ducts but more exposure to wind and snow, so the outdoor unit goes on a stand above the drifts. Closer to the lake, summer humidity is the bigger issue, and a variable-speed system pulls more moisture out of the air than an on-off AC. If you live in the lower city or on the Mountain instead, our page on <a href=\"/hamilton/\">HVAC in Hamilton</a> covers radiator homes and ductless options.</p>"
           "<ul class=\"checklist\"><li>Static pressure test and return air check before sizing</li><li>Manual J heat-loss calculation, not a guess from square footage</li><li>Indoor coil matched to the outdoor heat pump</li><li>240V circuit and panel review by ESA-licensed electricians</li><li>Thermostat set to hand off to the furnace when it gets truly cold</li></ul>"},
